// REJECTS: Unexpected token '3306'
//
// `port: 3306;` -- an integer literal -- in a MemSql datasource specification. MemSql types
// its port as STRING, so the ONLY spelling that parses is the quoted `port: '3306';` in
// ../fixtures/t2-memsql.pure, even though the value is then converted to an int at parse time
// (see ./neg-memsql-port-not-numeric.pure).
//
// The obvious spelling is the wrong one, and the vendors do not agree: Trino, Redshift,
// Oracle, Aurora and Spanner all type their ports/proxyPorts as INTEGER and reject the quoted
// form, while MemSql and Databricks type theirs as STRING and reject the bare form. Nine
// grammars, one field name, two incompatible token types, no rule for telling which is which
// short of reading each .g4.
//
// A rewrite that normalises this -- accepting either spelling everywhere, which costs nothing
// and looks like a kindness -- is more permissive than legend-engine in both directions at
// once. This fixture pins one direction; ./neg-trino-port-string.pure pins the other.
###Connection
RelationalDatabaseConnection fixture::neg::MemSqlIntPort
{
   store: fixture::neg::Db;
   type: MemSQL;
   specification: MemSql
   {
      host: 'localhost';
      port: 3306;
   };
   auth: Test;
}
